---
import { renderToString } from "katex";
import ElmanStateLedger from "./ElmanStateLedger.astro";
import EvidenceDetails from "./EvidenceDetails.astro";
import JourneyMap from "./JourneyMap.astro";
import ModeSwitch from "./ModeSwitch.astro";
import { getJourneyOverview, getStageView, type StageView } from "../lib/site-data";
interface Props { view: StageView }
const { view } = Astro.props;
const { mode } = view;
const slug = view.stage.slug;
const scenario = view.elmanScenario;
if (scenario === undefined) throw new Error("Elman is missing its reviewed scenario.");
const journey = getJourneyOverview("recurrent-memory");
const equation = renderToString(String.raw`h_t=\sigma(w_xx_t+w_hh_{t-1}+b)`, { displayMode: true, output: "htmlAndMathml", throwOnError: true });
const sigmoid = renderToString(String.raw`\sigma(z)=\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}`, { displayMode: true, output: "htmlAndMathml", throwOnError: true });
---

  <article class="stage-shell">
    <div class="stage-reading">
      <ModeSwitch activeMode={mode} alternateHref={view.alternateMode.href} />
      <header class="stage-header">
        <p class="stage-number">Stage 1 of 6 · report 1988 · journal 1990</p>
        <h1>{mode === "beginner" ? "Can the same word leave a different state?" : "What is copied—and what is learned?"}</h1>
        <p class="stage-question">{view.contribution}</p>
        <dl class="stage-meta"><div><dt>Work</dt><dd>{view.work.title} · {view.work.authors.join(", ")}</dd></div><div><dt>Display source</dt><dd>{view.canonicalVersion.label}</dd></div><div><dt>Chronology</dt><dd>Earlier CRL report: April 1988; journal: March 1990</dd></div><div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Carried hidden activation · no gated cell</dd></div></dl>
      </header>
      <section class="objective-band" aria-labelledby="objective-heading"><h2 id="objective-heading">Your task</h2><p>{view.objective.objective}</p><details><summary>Success criterion</summary><p>{view.objective.successCriterion}</p></details></section>
      <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="copy-path-heading">
        <p class="kicker">Mechanism inventory · 1990</p><h2 id="copy-path-heading">Copy, then transform.</h2>
        <ol class="copy-path"><li><strong>Carry</strong><span>Previous hidden activations → context units. Fixed one-to-one copy, weight 1.0.</span></li><li><strong>Transform</strong><span>Current input + copied context → new hidden activations. These upward paths are trainable.</span></li></ol>
        <p class="mt-3">The copied values are activation, not learned weights. The trainable paths are held fixed while you inspect the teaching trace.</p>
        <EvidenceDetails evidence={view.evidence.find((item) => item.claimId === "elman-1990-simple-recurrent-context")!} />
      </section>
      {mode === "expert" && <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="teaching-notation"><p class="kicker">Atlas reconstruction · not a source equation</p><h2 id="teaching-notation">A scalar update you can audit</h2><div class="equation-panel" role="region" aria-label="Scalar teaching recurrence" tabindex="0"><div set:html={equation} /><div set:html={sigmoid} /></div><p>The copied context is h from the previous step. The fixed copy has weight 1.0; wₕ = {scenario.recurrentWeight} belongs to the separate trainable return path. Here wₓ = {scenario.inputWeight}, b = {scenario.bias}, and h₀ = {scenario.initialState}. These authored scalar values simplify a distributed hidden layer.</p><p class="provenance-note mt-3">Source: printed pp. 182–184 and Figure 2 describe context units, hidden units, fixed copying, and trainable connections in prose and a diagram. No compact scalar recurrence is printed there.</p></section>}
      {view.blocks.map((block) => <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby={block.id}><p class="kicker">{block.kind}</p><h2 id={block.id}>{block.heading}</h2><p>{block.body}</p>{block.evidence.map((evidence) => <EvidenceDetails evidence={evidence} />)}</section>)}
    </div>
    <aside class="stage-workbench stage-workbench--long" aria-label="Static recurrent-state comparison"><ElmanStateLedger scenario={scenario} mode={mode} /></aside>
    <div class="stage-conclusion">
      {mode === "expert" && <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="experiment-envelope"><p class="kicker">Static evidence · printed pp. 195–198</p><h2 id="experiment-envelope">Read the metric within the experiment.</h2><p>The generated lexical task uses 29 word types, 10,000 short sentences, 31-bit one-hot inputs, 150 hidden and 150 context units, and six training passes. The successor-frequency comparison reports RMS 0.053 (SD 0.100) and cosine 0.916 (SD 0.123) on training items.</p><p class="provenance-note mt-3">Those figures are neither held-out perplexity nor a normalized language-model score. The scan disagrees on total token count (27,534 / 27,354 / 27,454); no count is silently selected. Type averaging, the unshown all-token dendrogram, and ZOG’s frozen-weight substitution remain qualified in the evidence above.</p></section>}
      <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="scope-heading"><p class="kicker">Boundary</p><h2 id="scope-heading">History dependence has limits.</h2><p>{mode === "beginner" ? "The running-summary analogy stops at perfect recall. Repeated updates can lose earlier information. A larger state value is not more memory, more certainty, or a probability." : "Current-step error updates trainable paths locally; this is not full backpropagation through earlier copied states. Forward dependence on history does not establish an equally long backward credit path."}</p><p class="mt-3">Elman credits Jordan’s earlier recurrent-state proposal and changes the feedback source from outputs to hidden activations. This chapter does not establish the origin of recurrence.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={view.evidence.find((item) => item.claimId === "elman-1990-memory-boundary")!} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={getStageView("expert", slug, "recurrent-memory").evidence.find((item) => item.claimId === "elman-1990-jordan-attribution")!} /><a class="claim-link" href={`/reference/works/${view.work.id}/`}>Inspect the complete ten-claim work record</a></section>
      <nav class="reading-block" aria-label="Recurrent journey progression"><p class="kicker">Next distinction · chapter 2</p><h2>The original LSTM cell · 1995/1997</h2><p>The next chapter asks what changes when a cell protects a local credit path and gates writing and exposure. Its forget gate and peepholes belong to later works.</p><p class="provenance-note mt-3">Three later chapters and both bridge panels remain queued.</p><a class="button" href={view.next!.href}>Continue to the original LSTM cell <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><p class="mt-3"><a class="claim-link" href={`/journeys/recurrent-memory/${mode}/`}>Return to the {mode} route map</a></p><div class="mt-4"><JourneyMap journey={journey} currentSlug={slug} mode={mode} compact /></div></nav>
    </div>
  </article>
